echo "==> em-dashes"
# calef's standing style rule (CLAUDE.md): no em-dashes, use commas, periods, semicolons or
# parentheses. It is a house style rather than a correctness property, and it is gated for the same
# reason the roadmap's status vocabulary is: a rule nobody checks is a rule that decays. 419 of them
# had accumulated across 75 files by 2026-08-01 despite being written down from the start, because
# every individual one looked fine in the moment it was typed.
#
# Vendored trees are exempt: they are somebody else's prose and must stay byte-identical to the pin.
# The character is built from its UTF-8 bytes rather than written literally, because a checker that
# contains the thing it forbids flags itself. That is not a cute trick, it is the only way this gate
# can be self-consistent without an exemption, and an exemption would be a hole someone later widens.
#
# **A blockquote line is quoted material, not the author's prose, and the rule does not reach it**
# (calef, 2026-09-22: *"Quotes violating our style rules is acceptable. But not our own prose."*).
# This is a statement about what the rule was always for rather than a hole in it. The rule exists
# so prose here reads as a person wrote it, and a quotation is by definition not the quoter's
# writing; a tree that cannot reproduce a source's punctuation has to paraphrase it, and
# paraphrasing a quotation is the failure this tree fears most. It is deliberately NOT an exemption
# for any file, directory or corpus: a lane proposed exactly that, one exclusion per gate for the
# review transcripts it had gathered, and calef refused it (*"I don't want to change the style rules"*)
# because a rule with an exception per corpus is a list. This clause has no corpus in it and applies
# to every file forever.
#
# **What it cannot see, stated plainly because the next person to meet a failing em-dash gate reads
# this comment and not a commit message:**
#
#   - **An inline quotation inside a sentence still fails, and that is the intended answer.** A line
#     that mixes the author's words with a quotation is the author's prose, so the fix is to put the
#     quotation on its own `>` line. Matching quotation marks instead was considered and refused:
#     "any line containing a quote mark" lets anyone past the rule by adding quotes around their own
#     sentence, and no check can tell a real quotation from an invented one.
#   - **`>` is the only signal, so it cannot distinguish a quotation from any other blockquote.**
#     A blockquote used for emphasis or for an aside gets the exemption too. That is accepted: `>`
#     changes how every reader sees the line, so nothing can be smuggled through it silently, which
#     is the property that matters more than precision here.
#   - **It is line-oriented.** An em-dash on the second line of a multi-line quotation is exempt only
#     if that line also starts with `>`, which is how Markdown blockquotes are written anyway.
#   - **It assumes no tracked path contains a colon**, because it filters `grep -n` output by
#     position. No path in this tree does; one added later would slip past this filter silently.
#
# Anything a quotation cannot carry belongs in a file no prose gate reads, which is why the 55
# transcripts behind milestone 521 (does an AI review of a pull request catch anything the gates and
# the maintainer do not) are archived in `notes/delegated-review/transcripts.tar.gz` rather than
# committed as files.
emdash="$(printf '\342\200\224')"
# `path:line:text`, minus the lines whose text begins a blockquote.
emdash_hits() {
    git ls-files | grep -v '^vendor/' | grep -v '^patches/' \
        | xargs grep -n "$emdash" 2>/dev/null \
        | grep -v -E '^[^:]*:[0-9]+:[[:space:]]*>'
}
if emdash_hits | grep -q .; then
    echo "lint: em-dashes found (CLAUDE.md: use commas, periods, semicolons or parentheses;" >&2
    echo "      a quotation on its own '>' line may keep its source's punctuation):" >&2
    emdash_hits | sed 's/^/  /' >&2
    exit 1
fi
echo "em-dashes: none outside vendored trees and quoted blockquote lines"

echo "==> shellcheck"
# The 37 `#!/bin/sh` scripts, gated at warning severity. Two reasons this earns a dependency here
# rather than being generic hygiene:
#
# 1. **macOS dev, Ubuntu CI.** `/bin/sh` is bash-in-POSIX-mode on one and dash on the other, so a
#    bashism runs fine locally and fails on a runner. ShellCheck knows POSIX sh from bash and says
#    which is which. We currently have zero such findings, which is exactly the property that
#    regresses silently.
# 2. **These scripts ARE the gates.** script/fmt shipped for months accepting `--check` and silently
#    ignoring it, and unformatted code reached main behind a clean report. A gate that fails open is
#    worse than no gate, and shell is where failing open is easiest.
#
# Severity is `warning`, not `note`, and that is a deliberate scope rather than laziness (DECISIONS
# §38: a suppression is scoped and carries a reason). The tree has ZERO errors and ZERO warnings, so
# this is green today and is a regression guard. The 13 note-level findings are all SC2086 in the
# QEMU runners' argument assembly, where `$INITRD`/`$DISK`/`$NET`/`$GPU`/`$KBD`/`$MON` are unquoted
# ON PURPOSE: each holds a multi-word QEMU fragment, and quoting would pass it as one argument and
# break the emulator. Raising to note level means restructuring that assembly with `set --` (the
# pattern script/fmt uses) rather than blanket-disabling the rule, and that is a change to the script
# every test run depends on, so it is not worth bundling here.
# Dependency direction: nothing under crates/ may depend on a BINARY (kernel, user, xtask).
#
# This is the method, not a tidiness rule. The whole approach is "pure logic in crates that compile
# for the host, plus Kani harnesses over them" (CLAUDE.md), and both halves die the moment a crate
# reaches upward: `kernel` and `user` only build for aarch64/riscv64, so a crate that depends on
# either stops compiling for the host, and a crate that does not compile for the host is unreachable
# by `cargo test` and by `cargo kani`. The crate would still BUILD, in the bare-metal pass, so
# nothing existing would fail. It would simply stop being verifiable, quietly.
#
# That is the same failure Rule 7 retired for `#[path]` modules, one level out: an arrangement that
# compiles fine and opts out of the tests and the proofs. Zero violations today; this is a ratchet,
# the same shape as the shellcheck gate below.
#
# `user_mode_runtime` is deliberately NOT in the forbidden set even though it is EL0 syscall `asm!`: it lives
# in crates/ and is a legitimate dependency for other EL0 crates.
echo "==> dependency direction"
upward=""
for f in crates/*/Cargo.toml; do
    # Match a dependency KEY at the start of a line (`kernel = ...`, `kernel.workspace = ...`) or a
    # path dependency naming one of the binary directories. Not a bare grep for the word, which
    # would hit every prose comment mentioning the kernel, and these files are full of those.
    hit=$(grep -nE '^[[:space:]]*(kernel|user|xtask)[[:space:]]*(=|\.)|path[[:space:]]*=[[:space:]]*"\.\./\.\./(kernel|user|xtask)"' "$f" || true)
    [ -n "$hit" ] && upward="$upward
$f:$hit"
done
if [ -n "$upward" ]; then
    echo "lint: a crate under crates/ depends on a binary, which takes it out of host tests and Kani:" >&2
    echo "$upward" >&2
    echo "" >&2
    echo "Move the shared definition INTO crates/ and depend on it from both sides (Rule 7)." >&2
    exit 1
fi
echo "dependency direction: $(ls -d crates/*/ | wc -l | tr -d ' ') crates, none depends on a binary"

echo "==> package boundaries"
# Milestone 611 (every program and crate belongs to a package, and every package has a home). calef
# ruled on 2026-09-27 (pull request #1389, its decision's fork 3) that the tree stays one repository for now
# with package boundaries "drawn and enforced inside it by lint check", and that the end state is
# everything leaving it, with no default of staying. The rules, all over the tree and none a list:
# every tracked path has exactly one home; every crate and program is in exactly one package; a link
# across a package boundary goes to an `interface` crate, to a declared `depends`, or through a
# dated exception that fails once its link is gone. helpers/packages.py's header is the manual and
# notes/package-boundaries.md the table. The selftest plants one violation per rule first, because
# a green check that could not have gone red says nothing.
python3 helpers/packages.py --selftest
# Milestone 724 (the merge queue reports its ejection share and its time to merge): the daily
# aggregation, the share, the report's threshold and the CSV upsert, against fixtures with no
# network. The fetch itself runs only in the metrics and trunk-health workflows.
python3 helpers/merge_queue_share.py --selftest >/dev/null
# The verify shard timings (lane/verify-shard-refresh, 2026-10-07 UTC): script/verify's packer and
# its partition assertion, the per-run drift warning's threshold, the log reader, the weekly
# refresh's drift rule and table rewrite, against fixtures and the live table, with no network.
python3 helpers/verify_times.py --selftest >/dev/null
# Milestone 808 (every gate accounts for its time): the CI job record's display-name mapping and
# daily rows against a recorded run, and the near-budget issue's three transitions (open, update,
# close after three runs under), with no network. The fetch and the sync run only in metrics.yml.
python3 helpers/ci_job_times.py --selftest >/dev/null
